WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.635 --> 00:00:02.736
-You know, our next guest
from her work

00:00:02.736 --> 00:00:03.971
on "Russian Doll"
and "The Morning Show."

00:00:03.971 --> 00:00:06.039
Her new film,"Past Lives,"
is available everywhere.

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:07.474
Now let’s take a look...

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:09.643
-[ Speaking in Korean ]

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:14.581
[ Speaking in Korean ]

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:16.416
[ Speaking in Korean ]

00:00:19.186 --> 00:00:20.521
[ Speaking in Korean ]

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:24.458
[ Speaking in Korean ]

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:29.263
-[ Speaking in Korean ]

00:00:31.098 --> 00:00:34.401
[ Speaking in Korean ]

00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:41.141
♪♪

00:00:41.141 --> 00:00:43.010
-[ Speaking in Korean ]

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.278
-[ Chuckles ]

00:00:44.278 --> 00:00:46.847
-Please welcome to the show
Greta Lee, everyone.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.850
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:56.523
♪♪

00:00:56.523 --> 00:01:03.163
♪♪

00:01:03.163 --> 00:01:04.665
Hi, Greta.
-Hi.

00:01:04.665 --> 00:01:05.766
-I am so happy
you’re here.

00:01:05.766 --> 00:01:07.000
I love this movie so much.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.702
-Oh. Thank you. You saw it.

00:01:08.702 --> 00:01:10.938
-I did see it.
Everybody has to see it.

00:01:10.938 --> 00:01:11.939
-Yes.
-There’s a moment --

00:01:11.939 --> 00:01:13.073
Have you seen it --

00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:15.576
much with, uh,
audiences full of people?

00:01:15.576 --> 00:01:17.044
-No, I haven’t.
-You haven't.

00:01:17.044 --> 00:01:19.112
-I really want
to watch it on the plane,

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:20.414
but I think that would be weird.

00:01:20.414 --> 00:01:21.582
-Oh, yeah?

00:01:21.582 --> 00:01:22.716
-Like if I did that.
-That's true. Right.

00:01:22.716 --> 00:01:23.951
-If you were watching it.

00:01:23.951 --> 00:01:25.786
-Yeah, if I was.
And crying at my own movie.

00:01:25.786 --> 00:01:27.054
-Yeah.
-I don’t know, I feel like that

00:01:27.054 --> 00:01:28.889
would be a little weird.
-It is a really good crier.

00:01:28.889 --> 00:01:30.123
And I should note,
this is not a movie

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:33.327
that is tragic in any way,
but it is --

00:01:33.327 --> 00:01:34.628
-I don’t know.
[ Sighs ]

00:01:34.628 --> 00:01:35.929
-it’s optimistically tragic.
-Soulful.

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:38.198
Optimistically, yeah. Tragic.
-Yeah.

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:39.266
-That’s a good way
of putting it.

00:01:39.266 --> 00:01:40.601
-Yeah.

00:01:40.601 --> 00:01:41.768
-There’s a moment in the film,
I think, you know,

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:43.170
and I don’t want
to give anything away.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:44.738
I would want to see it
in a theater if I were you,

00:01:44.738 --> 00:01:46.440
because I feel like
half the audience

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:47.875
would just go like,
[Shakily] "huh?"

00:01:47.875 --> 00:01:49.343
You know what I mean?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.343 --> 00:01:51.178
Like, that’s what I did.
-I think I know what moment

00:01:51.178 --> 00:01:52.513
you mean towards the end,
maybe?

00:01:52.513 --> 00:01:53.947
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-Or just at the beginning.

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:55.415
Right?
Right off the bat.

00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:57.518
-No, no, I had one at the end
that really got me.

00:01:57.518 --> 00:01:58.986
-Yeah.
And one of our producers,

00:01:58.986 --> 00:02:00.687
uh, who said, you know,

00:02:00.687 --> 00:02:02.422
Sarah said, "When I watched it,
just FYI,

00:02:02.422 --> 00:02:05.559
I watched it in my office
and I fully cried at my desk,"

00:02:05.559 --> 00:02:06.927
and I was like,
"Hahaha, Sarah."

00:02:06.927 --> 00:02:07.761
And I gave her a real hard time.

00:02:07.761 --> 00:02:09.229
And then...

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:11.431
And then I watched it,
and I fully cried at my desk.

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:12.566
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:12.566 --> 00:02:13.567
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:15.302
It can be a dehydrating
experience.

00:02:15.302 --> 00:02:16.837
-It’s very dehydrating.

00:02:16.837 --> 00:02:18.172
Now, this, uh, this movie,

00:02:18.172 --> 00:02:22.075
uh, well, again, it’s, uh --
-Made you cry.

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:23.510
-Made me cry.
-Yeah.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:26.013
-But you, uh, you play someone
who leaves a career

00:02:26.013 --> 00:02:28.048
when you’re 12 years old
and moves to New York City,

00:02:28.048 --> 00:02:29.583
and it’s about, uh, you know,

00:02:29.583 --> 00:02:31.018
someone you knew
when you were 12

00:02:31.018 --> 00:02:32.953
and sort of that reconnection
with somebody like this.

00:02:32.953 --> 00:02:34.855
This is a wonderful film
that was written

00:02:34.855 --> 00:02:36.924
and directed by Celine Song.
-Yep.

00:02:36.924 --> 00:02:38.592
-And how much of it
did you know?

00:02:38.592 --> 00:02:39.693
Did she fill you in?

00:02:39.693 --> 00:02:41.662
Was this autobiographical
for her?

00:02:41.662 --> 00:02:43.931
-Yeah,
it actually happened to her.

00:02:43.931 --> 00:02:45.799
She -- The seed of the movie

00:02:45.799 --> 00:02:49.469
was when she was actually
sitting between her,

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:53.407
her husband, her Jewish husband,
who lives in New York with her,

00:02:53.407 --> 00:02:55.409
and her actual
childhood sweetheart

00:02:55.409 --> 00:02:57.744
who had come to visit her
from Korea.

00:02:57.744 --> 00:03:00.447
Um, and she said
that that moment felt

00:03:00.447 --> 00:03:04.518
so, uh, hard to explain,
that it would take a whole movie

00:03:04.518 --> 00:03:06.019
just to explain
what that felt like.

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:07.788
-That’s fantastic.
-Mm-hmm. Yeah.

00:03:07.788 --> 00:03:12.993
-Now, uh, your parents, uh,
they immigrated from Korea.

00:03:12.993 --> 00:03:15.095
-Yeah.
-And, uh, were they excited

00:03:15.095 --> 00:03:16.597
that you were doing
a film like this?

00:03:16.597 --> 00:03:17.764
-No.
-Okay.

00:03:17.764 --> 00:03:19.600
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:20.901
-They were sort of like. Why?

00:03:20.901 --> 00:03:22.469
-Yeah.
-But you --

00:03:22.469 --> 00:03:23.770
And one of the things
you had to take,

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:26.507
you, uh, speak Korean,
but you had to --

00:03:26.507 --> 00:03:27.975
You took it very seriously,
and you wanted to speak

00:03:27.975 --> 00:03:29.676
Korean like somebody who left
when they were 12.

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:30.744
-Right. And that
was the whole thing.

00:03:30.744 --> 00:03:32.446
And that was part
of their doubt.

00:03:32.446 --> 00:03:33.847
They were like, you, you’re --

00:03:33.847 --> 00:03:35.315
you’re going to do
a full movie in Korean.

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:37.017
-They thought you weren’t
Korean enough to do it.

00:03:37.017 --> 00:03:38.719
-Yeah, they were
deeply concerned.

00:03:38.719 --> 00:03:40.187
They were like,
that’s our daughter.

00:03:40.187 --> 00:03:41.688
You know, we can’t let her
humiliate herself.

00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:43.156
-Did they, like,
call the director.

00:03:43.156 --> 00:03:44.324
And they’re like, you’ve made
a terrible mistake.

00:03:44.324 --> 00:03:46.059
-Almost. Almost.
-Yeah.

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:48.395
-No, I mean that, the language
part of it, it is --

00:03:48.395 --> 00:03:51.498
Okay. Anyone who speaks
two languages or is bicultural,

00:03:51.498 --> 00:03:54.434
that is such
a vulnerable thing to show.

00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:56.203
And language is so fluid.

00:03:56.203 --> 00:03:59.273
And, you know,
as you become Americanized,

00:03:59.273 --> 00:04:03.010
you -- you lose some
of your native language.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:05.979
And what we did was we worked
with this incredible woman,

00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:07.347
Sharon Choi,

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:09.850
who a lot of people know
as Bong Joon ho’s translator.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:11.852
-Oh, wow.
-She’s amazing.

00:04:11.852 --> 00:04:14.588
And because she can
translate jokes on the spot

00:04:14.588 --> 00:04:16.957
and that, to me,
is a true master.

00:04:16.957 --> 00:04:18.058
-Yes. Of course.
-Yeah.

00:04:18.058 --> 00:04:19.893
So we worked together
to figure out,

00:04:19.893 --> 00:04:22.596
like, if what a New Yorker

00:04:22.596 --> 00:04:26.133
who’s turned into sort of
like an annoying theater girl,

00:04:26.133 --> 00:04:29.269
like what her Korean
would sound like.

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:30.504
So maybe --
-That's really great.

00:04:30.504 --> 00:04:32.372
-Yes. It’s very specific.

00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:35.142
You know, only the most annoying
Korean theater girls

00:04:35.142 --> 00:04:37.945
know what that is,
and we need to honor that.

00:04:37.945 --> 00:04:39.146
-Yeah.
-Representation --

00:04:39.146 --> 00:04:41.114
It’s a very serious thing.
-Absolutely.

00:04:41.114 --> 00:04:42.950
And I think they would let you
know if you came up short.

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:44.251
-They would definitely. Yes.

00:04:44.251 --> 00:04:45.686
-That’s where the annoying
comes in.

00:04:45.686 --> 00:04:47.521
-Yeah. That’s right.
-So when your parents,

00:04:47.521 --> 00:04:48.956
for example,
first saw the trailer,

00:04:48.956 --> 00:04:51.959
did they think,
"Oh, my God, she did it."

00:04:51.959 --> 00:04:54.595
-They texted me
when they saw the trailer.

00:04:54.595 --> 00:04:55.929
They said,

00:04:55.929 --> 00:04:57.397
"Why is this in Korean?"

00:04:57.397 --> 00:04:58.832
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.832 --> 00:05:00.534
-So they just hadn't listened.
-Parents don’t listen.

00:05:00.534 --> 00:05:02.236
-No they don’t.
-They don’t listen.

00:05:02.236 --> 00:05:05.172
And now that I’m a parent,
I agree we don’t listen.

00:05:05.172 --> 00:05:09.376
-Uh, so your boys are almost
the same age as my boys.

00:05:09.376 --> 00:05:10.944
Are they --
Do they know what you do?

00:05:10.944 --> 00:05:13.514
Like mine are slowly coming to.
It’s dawning on them.

00:05:13.514 --> 00:05:15.215
-Yeah, they don’t
give a [bleep].

00:05:15.215 --> 00:05:16.617
-Okay.

00:05:16.617 --> 00:05:17.885
-Do yours?
-But I would imagine --

00:05:17.885 --> 00:05:19.286
-They don’t give a sh--

00:05:19.286 --> 00:05:21.722
No, they like that
people say hi to me.

00:05:21.722 --> 00:05:23.423
-[ Laughing ] Oh, okay.
-I think they like the idea.

00:05:23.423 --> 00:05:24.825
-The bar is really low
for them.

00:05:24.825 --> 00:05:26.093
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:26.093 --> 00:05:27.628
Well I think they like,
live in New York

00:05:27.628 --> 00:05:29.329
and they’re like,
nobody says hi to anybody.

00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:30.564
-Yeah.
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:30.564 --> 00:05:33.100
-Um, but you’re I would
imagine more now, right?

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:34.835
Because of this film.
Like, people must approach you

00:05:34.835 --> 00:05:36.803
and I’m assuming
you’re very nice.

00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:39.206
-Yes.
There is some crying, though,

00:05:39.206 --> 00:05:43.310
which might add to the confusion
and sort of like why my children

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:45.045
would be even
more dismissive of everything.

00:05:45.045 --> 00:05:47.114
-People come up to you and are
so affected by the film.

00:05:47.114 --> 00:05:48.815
When they say hello,
they start to cry.

00:05:48.815 --> 00:05:51.118
-Yeah, like imagine you’re at --
you’re at Trader Joe’s

00:05:51.118 --> 00:05:52.786
and you’re trying to buy cereal.
-Yeah.

00:05:52.786 --> 00:05:54.388
-And someone comes up
to you and they say,

00:05:54.388 --> 00:05:56.390
"Oh my God, like,
I just saw your movie."

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.358
And you’re thinking, great,
that’s so wonderful.

00:05:58.358 --> 00:06:00.027
And then they immediately
start to bawl.

00:06:00.027 --> 00:06:01.828
-Yeah, I mean, I imagine
that’s hard for you.

00:06:01.828 --> 00:06:04.598
I imagine that’s so traumatic
for a seven and a four year old.

00:06:04.598 --> 00:06:06.133
-It is, it is, it is.

00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:08.569
And I hate to say that they’ve
sort of gotten used to it.

00:06:08.569 --> 00:06:09.736
-Yeah.
-So.

00:06:09.736 --> 00:06:11.104
-Oh that’s --
But do they --

00:06:11.104 --> 00:06:12.739
I mean, obviously
you haven’t made a lot of work

00:06:12.739 --> 00:06:15.042
that is like very friendly
to seven and four year olds.

00:06:15.042 --> 00:06:17.144
Have they like --
Did they watch

00:06:17.144 --> 00:06:19.079
you even show them the trailer
for something like they.

00:06:19.079 --> 00:06:21.615
-Well, they saw --
My oldest, Apollo, is --

00:06:21.615 --> 00:06:23.350
is a very serious dude.

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:26.720
And he, uh, saw the trailer
and he was completely silent

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:28.889
the whole time
and expressionless.

00:06:28.889 --> 00:06:31.358
And when it finished,
he just said,

00:06:31.358 --> 00:06:33.060
"Looks like a good movie,"
and walked away.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.261
[ Laughter ]

00:06:34.261 --> 00:06:35.696
That’s --
That’s like, pretty good.

00:06:35.696 --> 00:06:37.030
In our household.
-That’s pretty good. Yeah.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:38.599
-If one of my kids
said that to me, I would --

00:06:38.599 --> 00:06:40.634
I would start crying like I’d
just seen "Past Lives" again.

00:06:40.634 --> 00:06:41.802
-Yeah, basically.

00:06:41.802 --> 00:06:43.070
That would be
my "Past Lives" moment.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:44.571
If they were like,
"That’s pretty good."

00:06:44.571 --> 00:06:46.607
-They also do this thing.
I don’t know if you have this.

00:06:46.607 --> 00:06:48.775
I mean it --
Winning awards

00:06:48.775 --> 00:06:52.846
is a very new kind of thing
for me as a concept.

00:06:52.846 --> 00:06:55.949
But how are your children
with the actual physical award?

00:06:55.949 --> 00:06:58.685
Because my kids are so critical,

00:06:58.685 --> 00:07:00.120
they want to know
they’re like, what is that?

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.523
They’re like, is that Sapphire?
-Yeah.

00:07:02.523 --> 00:07:06.560
-Like, no, it’s plastic.
They're like, "Oh, okay."

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:09.630
-Not to not to name drop.
We’ve got one Emmy in the house.

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:10.697
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:07:10.697 --> 00:07:12.699
-Um, I won it.
Not my wife and, uh.

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:13.934
-Important.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:13.934 --> 00:07:14.968
Important to get out there.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:14.968 --> 00:07:16.203
-But, uh,
they did have a moment

00:07:16.203 --> 00:07:17.337
where they were like,
they’ve just noticed it,

00:07:17.337 --> 00:07:18.805
and they’re like "Real gold"?
-Right.

00:07:18.805 --> 00:07:20.340
And I’m like, "No."
And they’re like, [Groans].

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:21.475
Yeah, like, [ Sighs ].

00:07:21.475 --> 00:07:22.976
-They're like,
"Maybe one day, Daddy."

00:07:22.976 --> 00:07:24.111
-Yeah. I’m like,
what are you --

00:07:24.111 --> 00:07:26.180
And if it was real gold,
what would you do?

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:27.614
-Melt it down.
-Yeah.

00:07:27.614 --> 00:07:30.450
They're immediately like,
"Your awards are short term.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:31.585
This is money, Dad."

00:07:31.585 --> 00:07:33.053
-Exactly.
So practical.

00:07:33.053 --> 00:07:34.855
-It’s so practical.
-Yeah.

00:07:34.855 --> 00:07:36.223
-It’s just such a beautiful
film.

00:07:36.223 --> 00:07:37.591
And congratulations...
Thank you.

00:07:37.591 --> 00:07:40.294
-...on, uh, your Critics Choice
nomination, and...

00:07:40.294 --> 00:07:42.229
-Yeah.
-Well deserved.

00:07:42.229 --> 00:07:43.530
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:47.201
-You know, it is.
But can I just say

00:07:47.201 --> 00:07:49.703
that I right now,
in this moment, I’m --

00:07:49.703 --> 00:07:52.039
I’m just thinking about
something totally different.

00:07:52.039 --> 00:07:53.874
And I’m carrying around
a lot of shame.

00:07:53.874 --> 00:07:56.143
I just learned
that I am responsible

00:07:56.143 --> 00:07:58.212
for committing a heinous crime

00:07:58.212 --> 00:08:00.781
because there’s something
called a Kimjang,

00:08:00.781 --> 00:08:04.117
which is this traditional event
where back thousands,

00:08:04.117 --> 00:08:06.019
maybe centuries
of years ago,

00:08:06.019 --> 00:08:08.522
Koreans would gather together
as a community

00:08:08.522 --> 00:08:12.259
with all of their hard-earned
resources and make kimchi.

00:08:12.259 --> 00:08:14.628
Right. So we do this event.
And this year,

00:08:14.628 --> 00:08:18.131
it happened to fall
right after the Academy gala.

00:08:18.131 --> 00:08:21.502
And Charles Melton came
with his mother, Sandra Oh.

00:08:21.502 --> 00:08:23.537
-Very good.
-All the fancy Koreans.

00:08:23.537 --> 00:08:25.239
And I just learned

00:08:25.239 --> 00:08:28.141
that I single-handedly
ruined the kimchi.

00:08:28.141 --> 00:08:29.810
-No, how?

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:31.845
-I didn’t wash it enough.

00:08:31.845 --> 00:08:33.514
-Oh my God.
-I know. But it's like --

00:08:33.514 --> 00:08:35.415
-I'm gonna act like I know
that’s bad.

00:08:35.415 --> 00:08:37.384
-Yes, should be like,
"Oh, my God."

00:08:37.384 --> 00:08:38.852
-You've gotta wash it enough.
-Yes.

00:08:38.852 --> 00:08:40.554
No, but, Seth,
this is really bad.

00:08:40.554 --> 00:08:42.990
It’s basically like I’ve killed
the whole village.

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:44.691
-Oh, my God.
-Yes.

00:08:44.691 --> 00:08:47.528
And it’s like the village has
decided, like, send her away.

00:08:47.528 --> 00:08:49.530
Let her be an actor.
It’s safer for everyone.

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:50.931
-You’ve been cast out?

00:08:50.931 --> 00:08:52.065
-Yes.
-Oh, my God.

00:08:52.065 --> 00:08:53.500
-I’ve been fired.
-Oh, my God.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:55.469
Well, I hope
they let you back in.

00:08:55.469 --> 00:08:57.137
Maybe if they -- if they haven’t
seen this movie and they see it,

00:08:57.137 --> 00:08:58.238
I think they’re going to
let you back in.

00:08:58.238 --> 00:08:59.540
-You think so?
-I do.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:00.707
-Okay.
-Thank you for being here.

00:09:00.707 --> 00:09:01.909
Will you, please,
come back soon?

00:09:01.909 --> 00:09:03.410
-I would love to come back.
-Oh, my God.

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:04.978
Greta Lee, everyone.
"Past Lives"

00:09:04.978 --> 00:09:06.747
is available everywhere now.
Go and see it.

00:09:06.747 --> 00:09:09.016
We’ll be right back
with chef Rick Martinez.

